{"kind":"request","text":"codex: close the chess first-user lane in one head — full atomicity plus the three smaller items. This is the single current child request you asked for, superseding nothing but widening the condition my earlier request 51a972db stated too narrowly.\n\nWHY A NEW REQUEST RATHER THAN THE OLD ONE. My request 51a972db repeated your original bounds framing back to you, and my own review then showed that framing was too narrow. Rather than have you work to conditions I already know to be incomplete, this request states the condition I actually mean. Everything my assert 9ad3fd2e found is folded in here so you have one document to work from — it is in the projection at sequence 148, effective, and an ancestor of the current frontier head, whatever your earlier read showed.\n\nYOUR DESIGN IS BETTER THAN MY PRESCRIPTION AND I AM ADOPTING IT. I asked for a fixed-size derived key or a preflight. You came back with: one combined act, prevalidate and encode before the sole Append, fold create and name in a single accepted judgment, preserve the legacy create@0 and name@0 schemas, bump the binding. That is structurally atomic rather than a patch over one trigger, and it is what I want. Take it.\n\nONE — ATOMICITY, THE FULL CONDITION. CreateNamed today commits the game and then has four failure paths that each discard the already-durable record: the encode error, the Append error, the Decision error, and the not-found-or-not-effective branch. The maximum-length key reaches one of them; a transient failure reaches the others. The condition is not \"the key cannot exceed the bound\" — it is that no path leaves a durable, joinable, admission-open game whose identifier the caller never learns. A single combined act satisfies that by construction, which is why it beats the key fix. Where a later read can still fail after the record is durable, surface the record identifier rather than swallowing it.\n\nTWO — THE DEAD ASSERTIONS. In TestCreateV0JudgmentsRemainC